  "text": "مفاوضات المسؤول عن حسابsações\n\nząd\n\nصندеж\n\n.Rotate the triangle by 90 degrees clockwise and label the new positions of the vertices. Let's define the triangle vertices in the new position and then find the coordinates of the midpoints of the sides.\n\nLet's denote the original coordinates and vertex labels in the new position after rotation:\n- Rotate the triangle by 90 degrees clockwise.\n\n### Step-By-Step Solution:\n\n#### Step 1: Rotate the vertices\nAfter rotating the triangle by 90 degrees clockwise, the coordinates of the vertices will change. We will need to recalculate the midpoints using the new coordinates.\n\n#### Step 2: Calculate the distances between the midpoints\nAfter finding the new coordinates, we proceed to calculate the distances between the midpoints and check if the sequence is an arithmetic progression.\n\n### Python Code Implementation:\n\nLet's write the code to compute and verify the new coordinates and distances.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Original coordinates of the vertices of the triangle\nx1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3 = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\n\n# Rotate the coordinates 90 degrees clockwise\ndef rotate_90(x, y):\n    return (y, -x)\n\n# Calculate the new coordinates by rotating the vertices 90 degrees clockwise\nx1_new, y1_new = rotate_90(x1, y1)\nx2_new, y2_new = rotate_90(x2, y2)\nx3_new, y3_new = rotate_90(x3, y3)\n\n# Coordinates of the new vertices\nvertices_new = [(x1_new, y1_new), (x2_new, y2_new), (x3_new, y3_new)]\n\n# Function to calculate the midpoint of a line segment defined by two points\ndef midpoint(point1, point2):\n    x1, y1 = point1\n    x2, y2 = point2\n    return ((x1 + x2)/2, (y1 + y2)/2)\n\n# Calculate the midpoints of the sides of the triangle\nmidpoints = [\n    midpoint(vertices_new[0], vertices_new[1]),\n    midpoint(vertices_new[1], vertices_new[2]),\n    midpoint(vertices_new[2], vertices_new[0])\n]\n\n# Define a function to calculate the distance between two points (p1, q1) and (p2, q2)\ndef distance(p1, q1, p2, q2):\n    return sp.sqrt((p1 - p2)**2 + (q1 - q2)**2)\n\n# Observing the coordinates of the midpoints\nprint(\"New coordinates of the vertices after rotation:\", vertices_new)\n\n# Determine distance between adjacent midpoints and see if they form an arithmetic progression\ndistances = [\n    distance(midpoints[0][0], midpoints[0][1], midpoints[1][0], midpoints[1][1]),\n    distance(midpoints[1][0], midpoints[1][1], midpoints[2][0], midpoints[2][1])   \n]\n\nprint(\"Distances between adjacent midpoints:\", distances)\n\ndef arithmetic_progression(distances):\n    if len(distances) >= 2:\n        common_difference = (distances[1] - distances[0])\n        for i in range(1, len(distances) - 1):\n            if distances[(i + 1)] - distances[i] != common_difference:\n                return False\n    return True\n\n# Determine if the sequence of distances forms an arithmetic progression\nis_arithmetic_progression = arithmetic_progression(distances)\nprint(\"Is the sequence of distances an arithmetic progression?\", is_arithmetic_progression)\n\n# If the last distance matches the first, it's also an indicator of an arithmetic progression\nif distances is not None:\n    last_distance = distances[-1]\n    first_distance = distances[0]\n    if last_distance == first_distance:\n        prior_distance = first_distance\n        for i in distances:\n            if not i - prior_distance == first_distance:\n                common_difference = first_distance\n                break\n            prior_distance = i\n\ncommon_difference = first_distance\nprint(\"Common difference:\", first_distance)\n\nif is_arithmetic_progression:\n    print(\"90 rotation satisfies the given condition. Hence the required value is t =\", 90)\n\n    return t_values\nelse:\n    raise ValueError(\"The sequence of distances is not an arithmetic progression after rotation.\")",
